WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.940
One of the most important theorems

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.500
that the SAS congruence condition helps us to prove

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.980
is the Isosceles triangle theorem.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.060
Before we move on to proving it,

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:17.400
we need to know what it exactly is.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:20.460
Look at the triangle ABC.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.440
These two sides are equal.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.660
Let's move the triangle to the left to understand the theorem.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:31.080
The theorem tells us that if side AC

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.080
equals side AB.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:36.500
That is, if this side equals this side,

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.720
then measure of angle ABC

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.020
will also be equal to that of angle ACB.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:46.020
This angle which is opposite to this side

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:50.860
will be equal to this angle which is opposite to side AB.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.060
If the two sides in a triangle are equal

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:58.000
then the angles opposite them are also equal.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:00:59.640
How do we prove this?

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:03.100
Let's move on to a new page.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.600
We redraw triangle ABC

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.460
and jot down all the data we have.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.500
Lengths of these two sides

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.940
are equal as it's an Isosceles triangle.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.860
The first thing we should do before we prove something

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:20.720
is write down all the data that has been given to us.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:22.140
We have been given

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:26.440
that segment AC is congruent to segment AB.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.480
The next thing we have to note down

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:31.080
is that we have been asked to prove.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.760
We have to prove that the angles opposite

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.540
the equal sides in a triangle are congruent.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:39.740
That is we need to prove

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:44.640
that angle ABC is congruent to angle ACB.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:46.320
Looking at the figure,

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.660
you would feel that we haven't been provided with any data.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.960
Then how are we supposed to prove these two angles equal?

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.060
The best way to prove any two angles or any two sides congruent,

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:01.720
is by proving two triangles congruent.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:03.940
So they become corresponding angles

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.500
and will hence be congruent.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.580
But here we have just one triangle,

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:11.800
and we need to prove these two angles congruent.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.640
In such cases we make two triangles.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.520
We can do a bit of construction to help ourselves.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:23.680
Let's draw the angle bisector of angle BAC.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.200
We are talking about this angle.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.260
So we draw the bisector like this.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.260
Let this red dotted line be the angle bisector of angle BAC

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.380
and let it intersect BC at Point D.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:44.120
Measure of angle BAD will equal that of angle CAD.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.720
Now we have two triangles,

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:51.040
triangle ABD and triangle ACD.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:53.020
If we somehow managed to prove,

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:57.160
that two sides and an included angle of triangle ABD

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.780
are equal to the corresponding two sides

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:03.140
and an included angle of triangle ACD

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.220
then we can say that the two triangles are congruent.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.880
After we have jotted down the data given

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:11.600
and the 'to prove' section,

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:13.840
we go to the proof.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:19.380
We have to look at triangle ABD and triangle ACD.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.860
Have we been given any data about the two triangles?

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:24.960
Yes, we have been given

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:29.460
that segment AB is congruent to segment AC.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.580
Based on our construction, we can also say

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:37.540
that angle BAD is congruent to angle CAD.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.920
Now this is interesting!

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:44.260
We can see that this side is common to both the triangles.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.920
We can say that segment AD is congruent to segment AD

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:51.280
since it is the common side.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:53.900
Guess what, we just proved

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.180
that the two sides and the included angles of both the triangles

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:58.560
are equal.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.400
Side-angle-side.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:05.380
We can say that triangle ADB is congruent

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:09.620
to triangle ACD by the SAS axiom.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.760
This in turn tells us that angle ABC

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.580
is congruent to angle ACB

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:17.580
since they are corresponding angles of

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:19.940
congruent triangles.

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:23.380
We prove these two angles congruent.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:25.720
We use the side-angle-side axiom

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.740
to prove the Isosceles triangle theorem,

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.600
which says that angles opposite equal sides

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:33.760
are always equal.

